For FY 2025–26, accountants should begin preparing annual GST reconciliation well before December.

Important reconciliation areas include:

- Turnover as per financial statements versus GSTR-1.
- Tax liability as per books versus GSTR-3B.
- ITC claimed versus GSTR-2B.
- ITC reversals and ineligible credits.
- Reverse charge liability.
- Credit notes and debit notes.
- Advances and adjustments.
- Exempt, nil-rated and non-GST supplies.
- Differences in tax payments.
- HSN summary and other annual return disclosures.

GSTR-9 and GSTR-9C applicability must be checked against the relevant exemptions and notifications.

## 3. Income Tax Return (ITR) Due Dates for AY 2026–27

Income tax return filing is another major statutory responsibility.

For income earned during FY 2025–26, taxpayers must file their returns under the Income-tax Act, 1961, selecting Assessment Year 2026–27.

The Income-tax Act, 2025 became effective from 1 April 2026. However, it does not mean that income earned during FY 2025–26 must be reported under the new Act.

### 3.1 ITR Filing Due Dates – AY 2026–27

| Taxpayer category | Original due date |
|---|---|
| Individuals not requiring tax audit – ITR-1 and ITR-2 | 31 July 2026 |
| Non-audit business/professional cases and applicable trusts | 31 August 2026 |
| Tax audit cases | 31 October 2026, subsequently extended to 21 November 2026 |
| Transfer pricing cases requiring Form 3CEB | 30 November 2026 |

**Important CBDT extension:**

Through Circular No. 07/2026 dated 28 September 2026, CBDT extended the ITR filing due date for specified audit cases from 31 October 2026 to 21 November 2026.

The corresponding tax audit report deadline for those cases was extended from 30 September 2026 to 21 October 2026.

As of 23 November 2026, these extended deadlines have passed.

### 3.2 Belated ITR Filing Due Date – AY 2026–27

Taxpayers who missed the original return filing deadline may still be eligible to file a belated return.

**Last date: 31 December 2026**, or completion of assessment, whichever is earlier.

Under Section 234F of the Income-tax Act, 1961:

- ₹1,000 where total income does not exceed ₹5 lakh.
- ₹5,000 in other cases.

Additional interest may apply depending on the taxpayer's tax liability and circumstances.

### 3.3 Revised ITR Due Date – AY 2026–27

If an individual or business discovers mistakes or omissions in an already-filed return, a revised return may be filed under Section 139(5) of the Income-tax Act, 1961.

For AY 2026–27, the applicable revised return deadline is generally before 31 March 2027 or completion of assessment, whichever is earlier, subject to the applicable amended provisions.

Accountants should verify the latest statutory position before filing.

### 3.4 ITR Forms and Their Applicability

| ITR Form | General applicability |
|---|---|
| ITR-1 | Eligible resident individuals with specified income |
| ITR-2 | Individuals and HUFs without business/professional income |
| ITR-3 | Individuals and HUFs having business/professional income |
| ITR-4 | Eligible presumptive taxation taxpayers |
| ITR-5 | Firms, LLPs, AOPs, BOIs and specified entities |
| ITR-6 | Companies other than companies claiming exemption under Section 11 |
| ITR-7 | Specified trusts, institutions and other eligible entities |

The applicable form should always be selected based on the taxpayer's actual income sources, eligibility and statutory conditions.

## 4. TDS Payment Due Dates for FY 2026–27

Tax Deducted at Source (TDS) is a mandatory tax collection mechanism under which specified persons deduct tax while making or crediting certain payments.

Examples include:

- Salary payments.
- Contractor payments.
- Professional fees.
- Commission and brokerage.
- Rent.
- Interest payments.
- Other specified transactions.

The deductor must deposit the deducted amount within the prescribed period and file the applicable TDS statement.

### 4.1 Monthly TDS Payment Calendar

For non-government deductors, the general due date is the 7th day of the succeeding month.

| Month of deduction | TDS payment due date |
|---|---|
| April 2026 | 7 May 2026 |
| May 2026 | 7 June 2026 |
| June 2026 | 7 July 2026 |
| July 2026 | 7 August 2026 |
| August 2026 | 7 September 2026 |
| September 2026 | 7 October 2026 |
| October 2026 | 7 November 2026 |
| November 2026 | 7 December 2026 |
| December 2026 | 7 January 2027 |
| January 2027 | 7 February 2027 |
| February 2027 | 7 March 2027 |
| March 2027 | 30 April 2027 |

**Special cases:**

1. Government deductors depositing tax without production of a challan generally deposit it on the same day.
2. March TDS for non-government deductors is generally payable by 30 April.
3. Specified challan-cum-statement transactions have their own filing and payment timelines.

### 4.2 Quarterly TDS Return Filing Due Dates

Quarterly TDS statements are important for reporting deductions made during the relevant quarter.

For FY 2026–27, the general filing schedule is:

| Quarter | Period | Due date |
|---|---|---|
| Q1 | April–June 2026 | 31 July 2026 |
| Q2 | July–September 2026 | 31 October 2026 |
| Q3 | October–December 2026 | 31 January 2027 |
| Q4 | January–March 2027 | 31 May 2027 |

### 4.3 New TDS Form Numbers Under the Income-tax Act, 2025

The new Income-tax Act and Income-tax Rules, 2026 introduce revised forms.

Some important form mappings are:

| Previous form | New form | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Form 24Q | Form 138 | Salary TDS statement |
| Form 26Q | Form 140 | TDS on non-salary payments to residents |
| Form 27EQ | Form 143 | Quarterly TCS statement |
| Forms 26QB, 26QC, 26QD and 26QE | Form 141 | Specified challan-cum-statements |

For FY 2026–27 transactions, accountants must use the applicable new forms and section references rather than automatically carrying forward the old section numbers.

For example, TDS obligations arising on payments or credits from 1 April 2026 are governed by the relevant provisions of Section 393 of the Income-tax Act, 2025. TCS is governed by the applicable provisions of Section 394.

### 4.4 TDS Compliance Checklist for Accountants

Before filing quarterly TDS statements, verify:

- TAN and deductor details.
- PAN of all deductees.
- Nature of payment.
- Applicable TDS rate.
- Threshold applicability.
- Date of deduction.
- Date of payment to the government.
- Challan identification number.
- BSR code and challan serial number.
- Amount deducted and deposited.
- Lower or nil deduction certificates, wherever applicable.
- Previous-quarter corrections.
- Reconciliation with Form 26AS and TRACES records.

## 5. TCS Return and Payment Due Dates

Tax Collected at Source (TCS) applies to specified transactions and categories of sellers or collectors under the applicable income tax provisions.

Examples include specified sales of goods, scrap, alcoholic liquor, overseas tour packages and other prescribed transactions.

### 5.1 TCS Payment Due Dates

The general monthly TCS payment deadline is the 7th of the following month for non-government collectors.

For March collections, the general deadline is 7 April.

### 5.2 Quarterly TCS Return Filing

The applicable quarterly TCS statement is filed using Form 143 under the Income-tax Act, 2025.

| Quarter | Period | Filing due date |
|---|---|---|
| Q1 | April–June 2026 | 31 July 2026 |
| Q2 | July–September 2026 | 31 October 2026 |
| Q3 | October–December 2026 | 31 January 2027 |
| Q4 | January–March 2027 | 31 May 2027 |

Accountants should reconcile TCS collected, deposited and reported in quarterly statements to ensure proper credit is available to the collectees.

## 6. Advance Tax Due Dates for FY 2026–27

Advance tax is payable by taxpayers whose estimated net tax liability after eligible TDS/TCS credits is ₹10,000 or more, subject to applicable exceptions.

For FY 2026–27, advance tax payments are governed by the Income-tax Act, 2025.

### Advance Tax Instalment Calendar

| Instalment | Due date | Cumulative percentage |
|---|---|---|
| First instalment | 15 June 2026 | 15% |
| Second instalment | 15 September 2026 | 45% |
| Third instalment | 15 December 2026 | 75% |
| Fourth instalment | 15 March 2027 | 100% |

These percentages are cumulative, not separate additional percentages.

For example, if the total estimated advance tax liability is ₹1,00,000:

- By 15 June: ₹15,000.
- By 15 September: ₹45,000 in total.
- By 15 December: ₹75,000 in total.
- By 15 March: ₹1,00,000 in total.

Eligible presumptive taxpayers under Section 58 generally pay the entire advance tax by 15 March.

**Important:** Interest for default or deferment of advance tax can apply under Sections 424 and 425 of the Income-tax Act, 2025.

## 7. Important GST and Income Tax Penalties and Late Fees

Accountants should not only remember filing dates but also understand the financial consequences of missing them.

### 7.1 GST Late Fees and Interest

| Compliance | General consequence |
|---|---|
| Delayed GSTR-1 | Applicable late fee under GST law |
| Delayed GSTR-3B | Applicable late fee and interest |
| Delayed GST tax payment | Interest generally at 18% per annum, where applicable |
| Incorrect ITC claim | Reversal and applicable interest or other consequences |
| Non-compliance with GST provisions | Additional penalties depending on the violation |

For GSTR-3B, the general late fee is calculated under the applicable provisions of the CGST and SGST Acts, with different limits for nil returns and other returns.

The commonly applicable general late fee is ₹50 per day in total for returns with tax liability and ₹20 per day in total for nil returns, subject to applicable statutory caps and exemptions.

### 7.2 TDS Late Filing Fee

Under the old Income-tax Act, 1961, Section 234E provides a late filing fee of ₹200 per day for delayed TDS statements, subject to the maximum amount of TDS deductible or collectible.

For FY 2026–27, check the corresponding provisions under the Income-tax Act, 2025.

### 7.3 TDS and TCS Interest

Common interest consequences under the previous Act include:

- TDS not deducted: generally 1% per month or part of a month.
- TDS deducted but not deposited: generally 1.5% per month or part of a month.

The applicable provisions under the new Act must be used for FY 2026–27 transactions.

### 7.4 Late ITR Filing Fee

| Total income | General late filing fee |
|---|---|
| Up to ₹5 lakh | ₹1,000 |
| Above ₹5 lakh | ₹5,000 |

Additional interest may apply depending on the tax payable and the circumstances of the delayed return.

